|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_four_graphs_

    Which function is most plausibly associated to
    which graph?

    |> table
        class=slice
        style=border-collapse:collapse;font-size:100%;text-align:center;margin:auto;margin-top:1em;margin-bottom:1.5em;
        |> colgroup
            |> col
                width=200
            |> col
                width=200
        |> tbody
            |> tr
                style=border-bottom:1px solid black;
                |> td
                    style=padding:0.5em 0;border-right:1px solid black
                    $x \ra \sin x \cdot \cos x$

                |> td
                    style=padding:0.5em 0;
                    $x \ra \cos^2 x$

            |> tr
                |> td
                    style=padding:0.5em 0;border-right:1px solid black
                    $x \ra \sin^2 x$

                |> td
                    style=padding:0.5em 0;
                    $x \ra \sin x + \cos x$

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_four_graphs.svg

    |> Solution
        Since
        $$
        x \ra \sin^2 x
        $$
        $$
        x \ra \cos^2 x
        $$
        are nonnegative [the square of a number
        is nonnegative]
        these functions must either be
        the top right or bottom left graphs, as the
        other two graphs weave in and out of negative territory;
        given additionally that
        $$
        \,\cos(0) = 1,
        $$
        $$
        \sin(0) = 0
        $$
        we can further deduce that
        $$
        x \ra \sin^2 x
        $$
        can only be the top right graph, and
        $$
        x \ra \cos^2 x 
        $$
        can only be the bottom left graph; moreover, since
        $$
        |\sin x \cdot \cos x| \leq 1
        $$
        for all $x \in \rr$ (as the product of
        two numbers that are between $-1$ and $1$ is
        itself between $-1$ and $1$), whereas the
        top left graph is at times greater than $1$
        and at times less than $-1$,
        !! steps outside the band $|y| \leq 1$, 
        the last standing possibility is...

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_four_graphs_labeled.svg

        ...this one! 

        |> SolutionNote
            These are in fact the actual graphs of
            the stated functions, not just “plausible” graphs.
            
        |> SolutionNote
            It is
            indeed quite striking that all of these
            functions seem to be _sinusoids_, given the
            appearance of their graphs. (A “sinusoid” is 
            formally defined as a function of the form
            $x \ra A\cdot \sin(Bx + C) + D$ for some 
            constants $A$, $B$, $C$, $D \in \rr$.)
            There is _a priori_ no good reason for this.
            !! A priori, there is no good reason for this!

